{#- _partials/_nonprintable_check.py.j2 — the non-printable character check
    (p64 R8.1/R8.2, task 2d.2).

    IT FLAGS. IT DOES NOT QUARANTINE. Every row containing a control character
    continues to the target; what this block produces is a COUNT, a log line, and
    (where a DQ report row exists) one `checks[]` entry carrying status
    `warning`. Nothing is diverted, `rows_quarantined` is untouched, and
    `overall_status` cannot move. That was p64 task 2d.1's decision and its four
    reasons are recorded in `_partials/_data_quality.py.j2`'s header rather than
    duplicated here — that is the file a reader arrives at when they wonder why a
    second row-level check does not reuse the quarantine write, so the argument
    lives beside the mechanism it declines to use. The short form: no DQDL rule
    can fail on a control character, so the quarantine write is not even on a
    live path for this signal.

    TASK 2d.2's OWN TEXT SAYS "feeding the same quarantine prefix and counters
    (R8.3)", AND THAT HALF IS SUPERSEDED BY 2d.1. Recorded here because the
    tasks file still carries the older wording and a reader comparing the two
    will otherwise think this partial under-delivers. R8.3 is written
    conditionally — "IF the check quarantines" — and R8.1 permits either arm
    ("flags or quarantines"), so the conditional simply does not fire. R8.3's
    "same report" IS honoured: the finding lands on the same
    `(run_id, "data_quality")` row, as a `checks[]` entry.

    WHY THE GUARD IS ITS OWN `quality_config` KEY AND NOT `data_quality_enabled`.
    This is 2d.1's reason (2) and it is the one that decides where this file sits
    in the render. `code_assembly.resolve_data_quality_enabled` defaults the DQ
    gate OFF for every template except `glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2`, so a check
    hung off that gate would render NOTHING for a JDBC- or Parquet-target
    pipeline that opted into it — an opt-in that silently does nothing, which is
    the defect class p64 exists to remove. `nonprintable_check_enabled` is
    independent, so the check renders on all five templates on its own terms.

    THE COST OF THAT INDEPENDENCE, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the two halves of
    this feature have different reach. The DETECTION renders wherever the key is
    set; the REPORT ENTRY renders only where a DQ report row is being written,
    i.e. inside `_data_quality.py.j2`'s `data_quality_enabled and dqdl_rules`
    arm. So a pipeline that opts into this check with DQ off gets the count and
    the log line and NO durable record of the finding — the row those checks
    would attach to is not written at all by that pipeline. That is not a gap
    this partial can close: the report row is a single `put_item` on a
    PutItem-only role (`LGR-9e76249-3`), so a second writer would clobber the
    first rather than merge with it. Closing it means giving the check its own
    row under its own `step_name`, which is a response-shape decision and
    therefore 2d.3's or a later task's, not this one's.

    ZERO RENDERED BYTES WHEN DISABLED, exactly as `_partials/_dedup.py.j2` is,
    and the whitespace arithmetic is the same one: the caller's `{%- include %}`
    strips the newline that ended the line above it, the outer `{%- if %}` and
    the blank line opening its body restore one when enabled, and
    `{%- endif -%}` strips the trailing one. Disabled, they cancel to nothing, so
    every pipeline that has not opted in renders a BYTE-IDENTICAL job and the ten
    stored `tests/fixtures/golden_scripts/` snapshots stay valid untouched. Do not
    tidy either side's minus signs independently — they are one expression.

    THE GUARD IS `is mapping and … is true`, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY **NOT** THE
    `is defined and` IDIOM `data_quality_enabled` AND `dqdl_rules` USE.
    `_dedup.py.j2`'s header carries the full account; the short version is that
    `is defined` was tried there, and FAILED, because
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s
    `test_dq_block_survives_a_second_jinja2_copy_in_sys_modules` renders all five
    templates with no `quality_config` and with two jinja2 copies loaded — under
    which `Context.resolve_or_missing` hands back one copy's `missing` sentinel
    while the compiled template tests identity against the other's. `_MissingType`
    is not an `Undefined`, so `is defined` answers True and the read behind it
    raises. `is mapping` is an `isinstance` check against `abc.Mapping`, which
    consults no sentinel at all, so its answer does not depend on how many jinja2
    copies are loaded. It also fails CLOSED in every case: unset, `None`, a scalar
    or a dict without the key all leave the check off, and nothing turns it on by
    accident.

    `.get(...)` for the same reason, and it is NOT the default-value filter
    do-NOT 6 forbids: that filter decides by asking whether its input is an
    `Undefined` instance, which is the identity question two jinja2 copies make
    unanswerable, whereas `.get` is a plain method on a value `is mapping` has
    already established is a real dict. That filter's name is deliberately not
    spelled anywhere in this file — the fence over it is a substring count across
    the whole file, so a header naming it would be counted as a violation of
    itself (`LGR-b46a5bb-4`). Both sibling partials carry the same note for the
    same reason.

    `is true` rather than plain truthiness mirrors `resolve_quality_config`'s own
    rule that only an explicit `bool` opts in, so the string `"false"` cannot mean
    enabled here either.

    THE CHARACTER CLASS IS C0 MINUS TAB, NEWLINE AND CARRIAGE RETURN, WHICH IS
    R8.1's OWN ENUMERATION READ LITERALLY: U+0000-U+0008, U+000B, U+000C and
    U+000E-U+001F. NUL, BEL and ESC are the three R8.1 names and all three are in
    it. Two things a reader will expect and NOT find, both deliberate:

      * DEL (U+007F) is excluded. It is non-printable, but it is not a C0
        control — C0 is 0x00-0x1F — and R8.1's parenthetical defines the class as
        "the rest of C0 except tab/newline/carriage return". Widening beyond the
        requirement is the kind of thing that is invisible until it flags a
        column somebody relies on.
      * The C1 range (U+0080-U+009F) is excluded for the same reason, plus a
        second: in a mis-decoded Latin-1 or CP1252 file those bytes are ordinary
        printable characters, so flagging them would report an encoding opinion
        as a data defect.

    The asymmetry runs one way, which is what makes the narrow reading the safe
    one: widening the class later is additive — a pipeline that flagged nothing
    starts flagging something, and nothing it already reported changes meaning.
    Narrowing it later silently stops reporting a finding an operator has been
    watching. So the class starts at exactly what R8.1 asked for.

    TAB, NEWLINE AND CARRIAGE RETURN ARE EXCLUDED BY THE REQUIREMENT AND ALSO BY
    ARITHMETIC: they are the three C0 characters a CSV or JSON source legitimately
    carries inside a quoted field, so including them would flag a large fraction
    of every text-sourced pipeline on its first run and the check would be turned
    off rather than read.

    THE PATTERN IS A RAW STRING IN THE EMITTED PYTHON, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
    `rlike` passes its argument to the JVM as a Java regex, so the `\x` escapes
    must survive Python's own string parsing to reach it. A raw literal keeps them
    literal. Written as a normal string, `"[\x00-\x08…]"` would make PYTHON embed
    real control characters in the pattern text — which happens to work for a
    plain range but is unreadable, and one `\x0B` away from a pattern that means
    something else. Do not "simplify" the `r` prefix away.

    ONLY STRING COLUMNS ARE EXAMINED, and the list is derived at RUNTIME from
    `df_transformed.dtypes` rather than from the pipeline's schema. Three
    consequences worth stating:

      * No identifier from the pipeline record reaches the emitted code, so this
        partial opens NO generated-code sink — there is no name here for
        `validate_identifier` to guard, and R8.5 / do-NOT 7's ordering rule is
        untouched by it. `LGR-819980f-1` (INVARIANT) is likewise unaffected: the
        only render variable interpolated is `quality_config`, whose members are
        two booleans and a list of already-allowlisted column names, and nothing
        is added to the `getResolvedOptions` key list.
      * A control character can only live in a string column, so a numeric or
        timestamp column has nothing to examine and skipping it costs no coverage.
      * A frame with no string columns at all runs no Spark action and reports
        zero, rather than building an empty predicate. `filter(None)` would raise;
        the guard is not decoration.

    ONE EXTRA SPARK ACTION, PAID ONLY INSIDE THE ENABLED ARM. The `count()` is a
    full pass over the frame. A pipeline that has not opted in pays nothing, which
    is the same bargain `_dedup.py.j2`'s two counts strike.

    WHERE THE CALLER MUST PUT IT: AFTER the DQ 'evaluate' include site and BEFORE
    `transformed_count`. Both halves matter and neither is a convenience.

      * AFTER 'evaluate', because that stage rebinds `df_transformed` to the
        Passed split (`df_transformed = _dq_passed_df`). Counting before it would
        count rows that are about to be quarantined and never written, so the
        number would not answer "how much of what I wrote is contaminated" — it
        would blame the target for rows the target never received.
      * BEFORE `transformed_count`, because the report entry is assembled in
        `_data_quality.py.j2`'s 'report' stage, which sits after that count. The
        local this block leaves behind must already exist by then.
      * It does not matter whether this sits before or after the dedup drop, since
        it neither reads nor writes the frame — but placing it after 'evaluate'
        puts it after dedup anyway, because dedup precedes 'evaluate'.

    THE LOCAL IT LEAVES BEHIND is `_adi_np_rows`, read by
    `_partials/_data_quality.py.j2`'s 'report' stage under the SAME guard
    expression as this file's. That pairing is the one thing to be careful of when
    editing either: the two guards must stay identical, or the report stage
    dereferences a name that was never defined. It degrades to a caught exception
    and a `logger.warning` rather than a lost row — the whole report write is
    inside `except Exception as _ddb_err` — but the finding would vanish
    silently, so `test_nonprintable_check_all_targets.py` asserts the include site
    exists on all five templates.

    NO STAGE VARIABLE, for the reason `_dedup.py.j2`'s header gives: this partial
    has exactly one emission point, so a stage argument would be ceremony with a
    second failure mode. The report entry is emitted inline by the DQ partial
    under its own guard rather than by a second stage here, which also keeps this
    file's whole context surface to the single variable `quality_config` — so
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py`'s `P64_PARTIALS` fence covers it without
    needing new exclusions.

    REQUIRED CONTEXT VARIABLE: `quality_config`, assigned unconditionally in
    `code_assembly.generate_code`'s `template_vars` from
    `resolve_quality_config`, which returns every recognised key on every call
    (R6.5 / do-NOT 6, design C7).

    ALL of the rationale lives in this Jinja comment and none of it in the
    emitted Python, per `LGR-b46a5bb-4` (template / ratified): this repo's
    template fences are substring assertions over the RENDERED output, so naming
    a fenced token in emitted Python would be counted as an occurrence of it.
-#}
{%- if quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("nonprintable_check_enabled") is true %}

    # === Non-printable character check (p64 R8.1/R8.2) ===
    # This check FLAGS: every row below continues to the target. It diverts
    # nothing, adds nothing to rows_quarantined, and cannot change the data
    # quality verdict. The finding is reported as a `warning` entry in the data
    # quality report's checks[] where that report is written.
    # C0 controls except tab (0x09), newline (0x0A) and carriage return (0x0D).
    # Only string columns can carry one, and the list is read off the frame at
    # runtime so no pipeline-supplied name reaches this code.
    _adi_np_rows = 0
    _adi_np_columns = [_adi_np_name for _adi_np_name, _adi_np_type in df_transformed.dtypes if _adi_np_type == "string"]
    if _adi_np_columns:
        from pyspark.sql import functions as _adi_np_functions

        # Raw string: the escapes must survive Python and reach Spark's rlike as
        # Java regex source.
        _adi_np_pattern = r"[\x00-\x08\x0B\x0C\x0E-\x1F]"
        _adi_np_match = None
        for _adi_np_column in _adi_np_columns:
            _adi_np_term = _adi_np_functions.col(_adi_np_column).rlike(_adi_np_pattern)
            _adi_np_match = _adi_np_term if _adi_np_match is None else (_adi_np_match | _adi_np_term)
        _adi_np_rows = df_transformed.filter(_adi_np_match).count()
    if _adi_np_rows > 0:
        logger.warning(
            f"Non-printable character check: {_adi_np_rows} row(s) contain control characters "
            f"across {len(_adi_np_columns)} string column(s) — flagged, not quarantined"
        )
    else:
        logger.info(f"Non-printable character check: clean across {len(_adi_np_columns)} string column(s)")
    # === End Non-printable character check ===
{%- endif -%}
